.TH CMP 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cmp \- compare two files byte by byte
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmp
.RB [ \-l | \-s | \-x ]
.RB [ \-bhz ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR skip | skip1 : skip2 ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR limit ]
.I file1 file2
.RI [ skip1 " [" skip2 ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cmp
compares
.I file1
and
.I file2
byte by byte.
Either operand (but not both) may be
.B \-
for standard input.
.PP
By default, when the files differ,
.B cmp
writes the position of the first differing byte to standard output
in the form
.PP
.RS
.IB file1 " " file2 " differ: char " N ", line " L
.RE
.PP
where
.I N
counts bytes from 1 and
.I L
counts lines of
.I file1
from 1, and stops.
If one file is a prefix of the other, the message
.PP
.RS
.BI "cmp: EOF on " name
.RE
.PP
is written to standard error, naming the shorter file.
.PP
The optional
.I skip1
and
.I skip2
operands give byte counts to skip at the start of
.I file1
and
.I file2
respectively; each overrides the corresponding value set by
.BR \-i .
Byte counts (for
.IR skip ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-n )
are decimal and may carry a suffix:
.B b
(512),
.B k
or
.B K
(1024),
.B M
(1024\(ha2),
.B G
(1024\(ha3), or
.BR kB ,
.BR MB ,
.B GB
(powers of 1000).
Skipped bytes are read and discarded, so skipping works on pipes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-print\-bytes
With
.B \-l
or
.BR \-x ,
also print each differing byte as a character, using
.BI ^ c
for control characters,
.B ^?
for DEL, and a
.B M\-
prefix for bytes above 127.
.TP
.B \-h
Accepted for BSD compatibility and ignored; symbolic links are always
followed.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-initial= \fIskip\fP | \fIskip1\fP : \fIskip2\fP
Skip
.I skip
bytes of both files, or
.I skip1
bytes of
.I file1
and
.I skip2
bytes of
.IR file2 .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-verbose
List every difference, one per line, as the decimal byte position
followed by the two differing byte values in octal.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-bytes= \fIlimit\fP
Compare at most
.I limit
bytes.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing (not even the EOF message); report only through the
exit status.
.TP
.B \-x
Like
.BR \-l ,
but print the byte values in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-z
When both operands are regular file names (not
.BR \- ),
compare their sizes, less any skipped bytes, before reading them.
If the sizes differ, report EOF on the smaller one and exit with
status 1 without comparing content.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.PP
Of
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.BR \-x ,
the last one given takes effect.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The files are identical (within any skip and limit).
.TP
.B 1
The files differ, or one is shorter than the other.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, a file could not be opened, or writing to standard
output failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Test whether two files are identical in a script:
.PP
.EX
if cmp \-s old.bin new.bin; then echo same; fi
.EE
.PP
List all differing bytes in hexadecimal, with characters:
.PP
.EX
$ cmp \-xb a.txt b.txt
.EE
.PP
Compare two files, ignoring a 512-byte header on each:
.PP
.EX
$ cmp \-i 1b disk1.img disk2.img
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1),
.BR sum (1)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024, for the
.B \-l
and
.B \-s
options and the default output format.
The long options,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.B \-n
follow GNU diffutils;
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-x ,
and
.B \-z
follow BSD.
.SH BUGS
A skip count larger than a file is not reported; the file is simply
treated as empty from that point.
